this is a production of cornell  university  uh hi i'm bruce wright i'm a professor  in horticulture  and  my my work is all in great breathing  great genetics  we're at a no spray disease resistance  breathing block here  my responsibilities are to both develop  new grape varieties for eastern  viticulture especially for new york and  also study the genetics of underlying  traits traits of great importance such  as  such as disease resistance  winter hardiness  and  quality which goes hand in hand with the  two  so great breathing at  new york state agricultural experiment  station we're now cornell agritech began  around 1885  and initially the primary goal was to  breed table grapes  for many years it was a table grape  breathing program and later in the early  mid 1900s it was a seedless  table seedless grape breathing program  it wasn't really until 1940s or 50s that  the project turned to wine grape  breathing  and most of the effort now more than  two-thirds of the effort  uh is in wine grape breathing for  disease resistance heartiness  and factors that make it easy to  harvest and grow consistently  in new york so  we're standing at a no spray breathing  block that was  first planted in the late 80s early  1990s  and  in this block  we took  the  most  resilient  selections that we could find in the  program selections that were grown under  neglect  almost under no spray conditions but  germplasm that i found in the program  when i first started that was really  just held for use in germplasm  collections i scoured through that  material to find the most disease  resistant material possible and started  to grow it actively under no spray  conditions  we developed mapping populations we  developed in our program some of the  first genetic maps  that were published in grapevines first  with isozymes then with rapids more  recently with gbs snip based markers and  ramp seat based markers  so a variety of technologies have come  along to improve the mapping and the  work to locate genes for traits of  interest in the program  so  early on it was very empirical in  approach  if it was disease resistant in the  nursery it would make it out to a  permanent vineyard to this site it would  be tested  and crosses would be made with higher  quality grapes to combine quality with  resistance that came from the many wild  species that we utilize in the program  the vitus gen projects came along two  different usda funded  specialty crop research initiative  projects  of which i co-lead the second one i was  leader of the first one  and with the team of fantastic  scientists that we work work with  scientists from usda scientists from  across the country  we've developed a very directed approach  to stacking genes for disease resistance  and to studying genes for  that relate to fruit quality to acidity  and other characteristics of fruit  quality  i think the best instances i can  describe for you are disease resistance  genes some of which were  found within our project within our  vitisgen project some of which were  found here  located in  germplasm that we were  growing  here at cornell agritech  and  others of which we've acquired and  utilized  and have developed good marker systems  to identify  the macro systems allow us to um to  stack these genes together for use in  the program we would not possibly be  able to combine different sources of  resistance without having the markers  that we have now the markers and the  combination of genes to me are really  important to try to develop long-term  stability and durability of the  resistance genes we do know that over  time  the activity of these genes can erode  we've seen  some of these genes  uh that seemed ironclad at the very  start  now  uh though they are powdery mildew  resistance genes we've seen some powdery  mildew growing on vines that that we  know harbor these genes  not a lot it's still a very useful  source  but we do know pathogens can mutate and  overcome these resistance genes  so uh here we are june 15th and we're  wrapping up our crossing season uh we've  made  more than 20 different crosses i i don't  have the exact tally but i want since  it's crossing season grapes are  flowering all around us  these are grape flowers  not the easiest things to work with  they're very small and they are perfect  flowers they have both viable pollen as  well as  stigma style ovary  and almost all cultivated grapes have  perfect flowers  but in the wild vines  of each species are either male or  female  within the vitisgen project my  colleagues  zoo chang  chi sun landscape davidson jason londo  have studied the structure of the  hermaphrodite locus the flower sex locus  and grapes and the paper was just  published  a couple of months ago in proceedings of  the national academy of sciences based  on this study which to to me tells you a  lot about the domestication of grapes  and the fact that there were two  different  instances of um  the  of the  arising of hermaphrodite flowers in  these species that either were male  vines or female vines so  take a look for that paper if you're  interested in an in-depth  uh analysis  this particular dead vine came to me as  a seed stock from hungary  where a muscadine grape  from down in southern united states a 40  chromosome species was hybridized in the  1920s early 1920s late teens or so over  a century ago to make a cross with bunch  grapes  most of our grape species have 38  chromosomes so very difficult cross to  make some viable progeny were obtained  and later  found their way to a lamb bouquet in  southern france who made further crosses  as he noticed that these  hybrids between muscadines and vinifera  had high levels of powdery mildew  resistance  studies were made the gene was located  it's a very strong locus for powdery  mildew resistance  adjacent to that locus is another very  strong gene for  downy mildew resistance  and  eventually that source i then utilize in  the 1980s and 90s  that source of powdery mildew resistance  which had now been introgressed into a  background which i could cross with  freely  found its way into some slightly more  winter hardy germplasm  which for a colleague of mine in hungary  paul cosma shared with me  and this is  some of the germplasm he shared with me  a vine that's struggling to survive  after mild winters  but the first year that it did produce  flowers after a very mild winter  i jumped on those flowers sight unseen i  knew the vines were powdery mildew  resistant  and i've been successful  in introducing this gene those two gene  locus powdery and downy mildew  resistance combined into more  uh well-adapted germplasm which i'll  show you next  so with the seeds that i obtained from  my colleague in hungary  the first couple of years they had a  chance to flower they did not again too  much winter damage but in 2006  they did flower  and i crossed them with other disease  resistant germplasm with reasonable  quality good winter hardiness and this  is one of the seedlings that then  resulted we have a plot here of six  vines of new york 06 5146  which is highly disease resistant it  combines by the scenarios ren2 locus for  powdery mildew resistance with the  resistance of the muscadine grape and  this is a red wine grape with some  chocolate and raspberry notes that's  gone out for trials more broadly beyond  cornell  so in this in this vineyard as you can  see we've placed qr codes here and  throughout the vineyard  and we're using  an imaging system a camera drawn on an  atv to image these vines  and one project is aimed at detecting  phylloxera on leaf tissue phylloxera  which is an important  insect has has in the past wiped out  european viticulture  and we're trying to envision phylloxera  on  leaf tissue because that can be a  problem with hybrid grapes  and using  the imaging system and algorithms to  pick out leaf phylloxera on tissues  we're trying to quantify that  in this mapping population and study the  genetics of phylloxera resistance  in addition we're cooperating with katie  gold an assistant professor in plant  pathology here at cornell on use of  spectral analyses  uv near-infrared visible spectra  analyses to to understand physical  physiochemical properties of leaf and  developing fruit tissues this location i  wanted to um to show you not necessarily  the oldest vine in the program but one  of the vines with the biggest trunk that  i i've ever grown  we just don't seem to grow trunks like  this in new york state this is a vine i  picked out early on  to initiate some of the disease  resistance breathing here it was growing  so well  uh winter hardiness-wise and disease  resistance-wise  it was growing so well under neglect  we've located uh from the by the  scenario parents of this  the ren2 locus for powdery mildew  resistance this is  the source source vine  for that study  and  this one happens to be a male so no  fruit quality to judge  but  we come such a long ways in the program  from the wild grapes that we started  with in the 1800s late 1800s  1900s  and to the present day  where we're now domesticating these wild  species and this uh rupestris by cenaria  hybrid the muscadine grape a couple of  uh species from from  uh china by this piazzesky by this roman  eddie eye  our sources of disease resistance genes  that we utilize  um  we're  bringing these into the program  and in the course of uh domesticating  these wild species  we've developed  somewhere over 60 different grape  varieties  in my time  at cornell agritech it's more than 10  different grape varieties that we've  developed mostly wine grapes but everest  seedless a jumbo sized  concord type seedless grape  was the last one we released uh probably  the first seedless grape in about 20  years to to be released from the program  it happens to be a tetraploid grape so  associated with having doubled number of  chromosomes you have a very large berry  size an average of five to seven grams  per berry  similar flavor to concord but twice the  size of a concord berry and seedless  wine grapes tramanet was one of our most  successful is a release from 1996  and  20 years later or so it was named  the outstanding fruit cultivar by the  american society for horticultural  sciences fruit breeding working group  it's  garnered major awards it's very similar  in wine quality to its coverts  tremendous parent so you could almost  not tell the difference with converts  tremendous yet it's much more winter  hardy much more disease resistant than  gewurztraminer  so that's been widely grown in new york  and elsewhere  it's great fun to go to the wineries and  see  tramanet selling at many wineries in the  finger lakes and elsewhere  in addition  some more recent releases include  aramella a highly aromatic aromatic  seedling of tramanette  very much more winter hardy even than  traminette and arendelle the first  cultivar that we named out of this no  spray breathing block a red wine grape  with high levels of disease resistance  and several wineries in new york kansas  and virginia are making very good wines  now with arendelle  each year each of these varieties is  responsible for between 5 and 15 million  dollars worth of wine production just  in new york state alone  so  so we see a  over time a cumulative economic impact  in the tens of millions of dollars  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu

