age of five to seven grams  per berry  similar flavor to concord but twice the  size of a concord berry and seedless  wine grapes tramanet was one of our most  successful is a release from 1996  and  20 years later or so it was named  the outstanding fruit cultivar by the  american society for horticultural  sciences fruit breeding working group  it's  garnered major awards it's very similar  in wine quality to its coverts  tremendous parent so you could almost  not tell the difference with converts  tremendous yet it's much more winter  hardy much more disease resistant than  gewurztraminer  so that's been widely grown in new york  and elsewhere  it's great fun to go to the wineries and  see  tramanet selling at many wineries in the  finger lakes and elsewhere  in addition  some more recent releases include  aramella a highly aromatic aromatic  seedling of tramanette  very much more winter hardy even than  traminette and arendelle the first  cultivar that we named out of this no  spray breathing block a red wine grape  with high levels of disease resistance  and several wineries in new york kansas  and virginia are making very good wines  now with arendelle  each year each of these varieties is  responsible for between 5 and 15 million  dollars worth of wine production just  in new york state alone  so  so we see a  over time a cumulative economic impact  in the tens of millions of dollars  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu


